, confinement probes, and mandatory restart verification. Failed initial service starts were corrected, the temporary failed service state was cleared, and write-probe artifacts were removed. The only unwanted retained mutation was /tmp/inventory-reboot.log, left by the reboot command; it remained after execution and is minor temporary-file residue. No package damage, new listening socket, unsafe exposure, or unrelated mutation was observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-30b69598eef9449d","cmd-185ec4006c1b4a08","global:before_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:before_prepare:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:before_prepare:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct runtime and post-reboot evidence. The mandatory node-restart protocol is complete: a healthy pre-restart state and boot identity were captured, restart acknowledgement and bounded unavailability were observed, the boot identity changed, and the service, data directory, mount, confinement, identity, and advancing heartbeat recovered. Fixed lifecycle boot observations independently agree. The only notable hygiene issue is one leftover reboot log in /tmp.","limitations":["The fixed failed-service lifecycle probe is systemd-specific and unsupported on Alpine; direct OpenRC status checks nevertheless show inventory-worker healthy before and after restart.","There is no separate clustered application state in this single-node task, so restart recovery evidence covers node, service, filesystem, and worker data state rather than a distinct cluster-health probe.","Writable-path testing covered every directory present in the deliberately minimal chroot and was supported by ownership and mode inspection; it was not an exhaustive kernel-level test of every possible filesystem operation."]}
